.TH FLEET-TRACE 1 "2026-11-08" "cti-agent 1.6" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
fleet-trace \- keep and read a record of what each heartbeat did, and what each model call cost
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fleet\-trace show
.RI [ FILE " | " \fB\-\-last\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-full ]
.br
.B fleet\-trace list
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.br
.B fleet\-trace prune \-\-days
.I N
.br
.B fleet\-trace init
.br
.B fleet\-trace unwrap \-\-result
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-trace
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B fleet\-trace tee \-\-out
.I FILE
.B \-\-plain
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-max\-mb
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-keep
.IR minimal | full ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent fleet\-trace
.I subcommand
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every orchestrator heartbeat that
.BR run\-checkin (1)
starts is recorded as it happens: the model and tools it started with, each
tool it called and with what, whether the call succeeded or was denied, what
it said, and at the end how many turns it took, the tokens it used and what it
cost. The record is the
.B claude
CLI's own
.B stream\-json
output, one JSON object per line, kept in
.IR $FLEET_HOME/state/traces .
.PP
Before traces, the only account of a beat was whatever the model chose to
print. A trace answers "why did the heartbeat escalate on Tuesday?" from what
it actually did.
.PP
By default a trace keeps what the beat did, not the tool results it read, and
works by allowlist. It records what the model wrote, every tool call and its
arguments, what was denied, the turns, tokens, cost and the final result. What
the model wrote and the arguments it passed can quote mail, so a minimal trace is
far less than a copy of the mailbox and is not nothing. Anything in an event
shape the program does not know is kept only as a size and a hash. A
tool result (a mail body, an advisory, a report) is replaced by its size and the
first 16 characters of its SHA-256, so "what did it look at" has an answer and
the trace is not a second copy of the mailbox. A failed tool call keeps its
first 300 characters, because the reason for a refusal is what an operator
needs. The model's private reasoning is kept as a length.
.B \-\-keep full
(set
.B CHECKIN_TRACE_KEEP=full
in fleet.env) keeps every event as it came, for an investigation; untrusted
advisory and mail text then sits in the file, so set it back afterwards.
.PP
Either way a trace is handled like a report: mode 0600 in a 0700 directory,
deleted after
.BR CHECKIN_TRACE_DAYS ,
never mailed, never committed. The beat itself cannot read it:
.B run\-checkin
grants no
.B Read
on
.I state/
and does not grant
.BR fleet\-trace .
.PP
.B show
and
.B list
replace every control character, ANSI escape and bidirectional override with
.B ?
before printing, so text the beat read cannot rewrite what the operator's
terminal shows.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR show " [\fIFILE\fR | " \-\-last "] [" \-\-full ]
Print one beat as a timeline. With no argument, or
.BR \-\-last ,
the newest heartbeat; name a
.B supplier\-
trace to see one of those. A
.I FILE
without a slash is looked for in the trace directory. Tool results are shown
as a size and ok or ERROR; with
.B \-\-full
their text is printed as well, sanitized, up to 4000 characters each.
.TP
.BR list " [" \-n " \fIN\fR]"
One line per heartbeat or supplier run, newest first by the time in the name:
the trace's name and size, ok or ERROR, model calls, turns, tool calls denied,
and cost (a supplier run's is the sum of its calls). A last line gives the
total for the rows listed, and per lane. Default 10; 0 lists all, which is the
fleet's model spend for as long as traces are kept. A trace with no result line
(the beat was killed) shows
.BR none .
.TP
.BI "prune \-\-days " N
Delete traces older than
.I N
days. Only files named
.BI checkin\- YYYYMMDD T HHMMSS .jsonl
or
.BI supplier\- YYYYMMDD T HHMMSS .jsonl
in the trace directory are touched, never through a link.
.B run\-checkin
runs it after every beat with
.BR CHECKIN_TRACE_DAYS .
.TP
.B init
Create the trace directory, mode 0700, and prove a file can be written in
it. Prints the directory.
.B run\-checkin
runs it before each beat and runs the beat untraced if it fails.
.TP
.BI "tee \-\-out " FILE " \-\-plain " FILE " \fR[\fP\-\-max\-mb " N\fR] [\fP\-\-keep " minimal\fR|\fPfull\fR]\fP
Used by
.BR run\-checkin ,
not by people. Reads the beat's output on standard input and does three
things with it: keeps its events in the new file
.B \-\-out
(created 0600, refused if it exists), prints the timeline to standard output
for the journal, and writes to
.B \-\-plain
only what the CLI's text mode used to print: lines that are not JSON
(standard error), API error messages, and the final result.
.B cti\-budget record
classifies that file, by words such as
.BR 429 ,
.B quota
and
.BR billing ;
the timeline's token counts or the tool output in front of it would turn
ordinary beats into holds.
.IP
.B \-\-keep
says how much: the default
.B minimal
drops the content of successful tool results (kept as a size and a short hash)
and the model's private reasoning, and caps a failed result and stray standard
error at 300 and 2000 characters;
.B full
keeps every event as it came. The trace's first line records which.
.B show \-\-full
on a minimal trace prints
.B [content not kept]
where the content was.
.IP
The trace stops at
.B \-\-max\-mb
megabytes (default 20) with a
.B truncated
marker; the reading does not. A trace that cannot be written stops the
trace, not the reading, so the beat is never cut off by a full disk.
.TP
.BI "unwrap \-\-result " FILE " \fR[\fB\-\-trace \fIFILE\fR]"
Read one
.B claude \-p \-\-output\-format json
reply on standard input and write the model's answer to
.B \-\-result
(created 0600, refused if it exists): the same text the CLI's text mode
prints.
.BR run\-supplier (1)
uses it for each request it judges. With
.BR \-\-trace ,
which must be named
.BI supplier\- YYYYMMDD T HHMMSS .jsonl\fR,
the call's cost is appended to that trace (created 0600 with a header line,
never through a link): the turns, duration, token counts, cost and model
names, never the answer, which discusses untrusted supplier mail. The cost is
kept even for a failed call, which is still billed; a trace that cannot be
written loses the record, not the answer. Exits 1 when the reply is not a JSON
result or holds no usable answer.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FLEET_HOME
The state directory; traces are in
.IR $FLEET_HOME/state/traces .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/traces/checkin\-*.jsonl
One file per beat. The first line is
.BR {"type":"fleet_trace",...} ;
a line the CLI printed that was not JSON is kept as
.BR {"type":"fleet_trace_text","text":...} ;
every other line is the CLI's own event.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
.BR show :
no trace, or it cannot be read.
.BR tee :
the trace or the plain file could not be written in full; the reading still
went to the end.
.BR init :
the directory cannot be made or written.
.TP
.B 2
Usage: an unknown subcommand or argument.
.SH EXAMPLES
The last beat:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-trace show \-\-last
.EE
.PP
The last day of beats at a glance:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-trace list \-n 48
.EE
.PP
One beat with every tool result:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-trace show checkin\-20261108T093000.jsonl \-\-full | less
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-checkin (1),
.BR cti\-budget (1),
.BR fleet\-journal (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
HARNESS.md, H1.
